Picked up your phone for one thing and came back 20 minutes later with a tight jaw and a buzzy head. That happens to most of us.

A small Durham study found that while people scrolled, their heart rate dropped but skin conductance rose, and when they stopped, stress went up. It didn't matter whether they were heavy users.

The fix isn't deleting the app. It's six slow breaths, mid-scroll: 4 in, 6 out. {url}

social.linkedin_post:
Most advice about feeling fried by your phone is about cutting minutes. The research points somewhere more useful: the state the feed leaves you in.

In a Durham lab study of 54 young adults, Instagram lowered heart rate while raising skin conductance, and stopping pushed both up along with stress. Another experiment found that batching notifications three times a day beat both constant alerts and turning them off entirely.

We wrote up what the evidence supports, where it's still early, and a 60-second mid-scroll reset (4 counts in, 6 out). Regulate, don't restrict. {url}
